Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero original dword I/O primitives

:in32[port] returns the 32-bit I/O word zero-extended to the language value.
!out32[port | value]. writes the low 32 value bits. Both use only the low 16
port bits, just like existing in8/in16/out8/out16. No implicit PCI discovery
or register policy is embedded in these primitives; drivers implement it.
Supported targets: raw64, raw32, EFI64, EFI32, and 386+ real mode.
Linux ELF, Windows PE and ZEROAPP1 application targets reject them before
producing output.

Metadata indices 54 and 55 append the names in32/out32, without renumbering any
old operation. Tokens 554/555 point to templates 82/83. Names are reserved in
all targets, including those rejecting I/O. Arity/effect forms remain strict.
Template 83 uses the existing binary operand-pop preparation. The port
primitives alone do not change arena sizes or parser limits.
The current merged compiler has 59 variables and a 491520-byte output buffer;
selfhost/CORE.ztext records the full current layout.

Original direct instruction encodings (not assembler input)
X64 in32: 89c2 ed              mov EDX,EAX; IN EAX,DX
X64 out32:89ca ef              mov EDX,ECX; OUT DX,EAX
IA32 in32:89c2 ed 31d2         read EAX, then clear high result EDX
IA32 out32:89ca ef             ignore high port/value pair words
Real in32:6689c2 66ed          move/read32 with operand-size prefixes
Real out32:6689ca 66ef         move/write32 with operand-size prefixes
The x64 IN EAX write zeroes the high 32 result bits. Tests independently read
0xffffffff from an absent PCI function and require the full u64 result to be
exactly 4294967295. IA32 explicitly clears EDX after consuming DX as the port.

Three actual QEMU probes exercise PCI mechanism 1 at CF8/CFC, not just emitted
byte patterns. A high-bit CF8 write/read roundtrip verifies dword width; a
440FX vendor/device read verifies all 32 data bits; high port bits are ignored.
The BIOS32 probe disables long mode and PAE. Real-mode high-port tests stay
within its u32 source contract. EFI variants compile the same reviewed native
instruction templates; no separate EFI execution claim is made by this test.

review-tests/network-ports/run.py records the original 384/448 KiB optical
probe layouts; it is not a current image-layout integration runner. Final
compiler-only target/parity checks are in review-tests/compiler-0.9/check.py,
with evidence in verification/compiler-0.9. Earlier actual VM probes used
private ISO copies; their emitted probe bytes are checked for exact parity.
No user disk is opened. No assembler, linker, borrowed compiler backend or
foreign guest runtime is involved. Python/QEMU are external test infrastructure.
